1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,773
This is ECE 358, video 10 a. The topic is TCP reliable data transfer: segments, ACKs, and retransmission.

2
00:00:11,223 --> 00:00:36,482
After this video, you can explain the fields of the TCP segment. You can find TCP sequence numbers and ACK numbers. You can trace lost ACKs, premature timeouts, and fast retransmit. You can apply the four ACK rules of the receiver. At the end, we solve question bank questions 37 to 39. Video 10 b covers flow control and connections.

3
00:00:36,932 --> 00:00:47,855
Slide 52 lists the features of TCP. First, TCP is point-to-point. One connection has exactly one sender and one receiver.

4
00:00:48,305 --> 00:01:00,230
Second, TCP gives a reliable, in-order byte stream. TCP gives a number to each byte, not to each message. Here, host A sends bytes 0 to 11.

5
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:12,413
There are no message boundaries. A segment can carry any number of data bytes, up to a maximum. Here, the 12 bytes go in three segments of 3, 4, and 5 bytes.

6
00:01:12,863 --> 00:01:24,661
Third, TCP is full duplex. Data flows in both directions in the same connection. Here, host B sends its bytes 79 to 84 to host A.

7
00:01:25,111 --> 00:01:31,980
The MSS, the maximum segment size, is the largest number of data bytes in one segment.

8
00:01:32,430 --> 00:01:42,073
Fourth, TCP uses cumulative ACKs. One ACK number confirms all the bytes before it. ACK 3 confirms bytes 0 to 2.

9
00:01:42,523 --> 00:01:56,752
TCP also uses pipelining. Many segments can wait for an ACK at the same time. Here, segments 2 and 3 are in flight. Congestion control and flow control set the size of this window.

10
00:01:57,202 --> 00:02:09,789
Fifth, TCP is connection-oriented. A handshake sets the state of both sides before the data: window sizes, MSS, and the initial sequence number, ISN.

11
00:02:10,239 --> 00:02:15,487
TCP is flow controlled. The sender does not overwhelm the receiver.

12
00:02:15,937 --> 00:02:26,219
And TCP is congestion controlled. The sender tries not to overwhelm the routers and links. Video 11 covers congestion control.

13
00:02:26,669 --> 00:02:37,976
Slide 53 shows the TCP segment. Each header row is 32 bits wide, numbered 0 to 31. 32 bits are 4 bytes.

14
00:02:38,426 --> 00:02:46,917
TCP is full duplex. So one segment can carry data from A to B, and also the ACK for the data from B to A.

15
00:02:47,367 --> 00:02:57,521
The first row holds the source port number and the destination port number. They identify the sockets. Video 7 used them for demultiplexing.

16
00:02:57,971 --> 00:03:05,609
The sequence number is the byte-stream number of the first data byte in the segment. It counts bytes, not segments.

17
00:03:06,059 --> 00:03:11,179
The acknowledgement number is the sequence number of the next byte that is expected.

18
00:03:11,629 --> 00:03:13,954
The A bit says that this segment is an ACK.

19
00:03:14,404 --> 00:03:22,425
The head len field gives the length of the TCP header. Its unit is 4 bytes, that is, one row of the header.

20
00:03:22,875 --> 00:03:28,422
So a value of 5 means 5 rows. 5 times 4 bytes is 20 bytes.

21
00:03:28,872 --> 00:03:34,205
The R, S, and F bits are reset, SYN, and FIN. They manage the connection.

22
00:03:34,655 --> 00:03:38,325
The C and E bits are for congestion notification.

23
00:03:38,775 --> 00:03:45,964
The receive window field is for flow control. It gives the number of bytes that the receiver is willing to accept.

24
00:03:46,414 --> 00:03:48,782
The checksum is the Internet checksum.

25
00:03:49,232 --> 00:03:53,861
The options have a variable length. One example is the MSS.

26
00:03:54,311 --> 00:04:00,050
Last comes the application data. The application wrote it into the TCP socket.

27
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:05,876
Slide 54 uses a byte stream of 12 bytes, numbered 0 to 11.

28
00:04:06,326 --> 00:04:11,553
The sender cuts the stream into three segments, of 3, 4, and 5 bytes.

29
00:04:12,003 --> 00:04:20,003
The sequence number of a segment is the number of its first data byte. So the sequence numbers are 0, 3, and 7.

30
00:04:20,453 --> 00:04:28,687
The ACK number is the sequence number of the next byte that the receiver expects. Segment 1 brings bytes 0 to 2.

31
00:04:29,137 --> 00:04:36,860
The next expected byte is 3. So the receiver sends ACK 3. That is sequence number 0 plus 3 bytes.

32
00:04:37,310 --> 00:04:39,763
Segment 2 brings bytes 3 to 6.

33
00:04:40,213 --> 00:04:46,421
The next expected byte is 7, which is 3 plus 4. So the receiver sends ACK 7.

34
00:04:46,871 --> 00:04:56,365
TCP ACKs are cumulative. When the sender gets ACK 7, it knows that all data up to and including byte 6 arrived correctly.

35
00:04:56,815 --> 00:04:59,652
Segment 3 brings bytes 7 to 11.

36
00:05:00,102 --> 00:05:03,835
The receiver sends ACK 12, which is 7 plus 5.

37
00:05:04,285 --> 00:05:11,795
The rule: the ACK number equals the sequence number plus the number of data bytes, when no earlier byte is missing.

38
00:05:12,245 --> 00:05:24,405
What does the receiver do with a segment that arrives out of order? Use the same stream. Segment 1 arrives in order, with bytes 0 to 2. The receiver sends ACK 3.

39
00:05:24,855 --> 00:05:29,975
Now segment 3, with bytes 7 to 11, arrives before segment 2.

40
00:05:30,425 --> 00:05:33,475
There is a gap: bytes 3 to 6 are missing.

41
00:05:33,925 --> 00:05:45,915
The TCP specification does not say what to do. The implementor decides. Choice 1: discard the segment. This is inefficient, because the segment must come again.

42
00:05:46,365 --> 00:05:50,205
Choice 2: buffer the segment. This is used in practice.

43
00:05:50,655 --> 00:05:57,801
In both cases, the ACK number stays 3, the first missing byte. The ACK number never jumps past a gap.

44
00:05:58,251 --> 00:06:02,753
Then segment 2 arrives, with bytes 3 to 6. It fills the gap.

45
00:06:03,203 --> 00:06:11,843
Bytes 7 to 11 are already in the buffer. So the next expected byte jumps to 12, and the receiver sends ACK 12.

46
00:06:12,293 --> 00:06:18,799
Slide 56 shows a simple telnet scenario. The user on host A types the letter C.

47
00:06:19,249 --> 00:06:25,180
Host A numbers its bytes from 42. Host B numbers its bytes from 79.

48
00:06:25,630 --> 00:06:31,070
So host A sends sequence number 42, ACK 79, with the data C.

49
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:41,312
Host B receives 1 byte at 42. It expects byte 43 next. So its ACK number is 42 plus 1, which is 43.

50
00:06:41,762 --> 00:06:50,957
In the same segment, host B echoes C back. This is byte 79 of B's own stream, so the sequence number is 79.

51
00:06:51,407 --> 00:06:58,319
Host A receives 1 byte at 79. So it sends ACK 79 plus 1, which is 80.

52
00:06:58,769 --> 00:07:05,809
Its own next byte is 43, so the sequence number is 43. This segment carries no data.

53
00:07:06,259 --> 00:07:10,781
The red curves link each sequence number to the ACK number in the reply.

54
00:07:11,231 --> 00:07:19,765
Each direction has its own byte numbers. The ACK number in a reply equals the sequence number received, plus its data bytes.

55
00:07:20,215 --> 00:07:34,913
Slide 57 gives the TCP sender. It reacts to three events. Event 1 is data from the application. The sender creates a segment. Its sequence number is the number of its first data byte.

56
00:07:35,363 --> 00:07:40,739
Here, host A sends sequence number 92 with 8 bytes, and starts the timer.

57
00:07:41,189 --> 00:07:46,971
Send base is the oldest unACKed byte. Here, send base is 92.

58
00:07:47,421 --> 00:07:55,975
Then host A sends sequence number 100 with 20 bytes. 100 is 92 plus 8. The timer already runs.

59
00:07:56,425 --> 00:08:06,239
TCP has only one retransmission timer, the R T O timer, per connection. It runs for the oldest unACKed segment.

60
00:08:06,689 --> 00:08:10,166
Its expiration interval is called TimeOutInterval.

61
00:08:10,616 --> 00:08:19,576
Event 2 is an ACK. ACK 100 acknowledges bytes that were not ACKed before. So send base becomes 100.

62
00:08:20,026 --> 00:08:27,237
Bytes 100 to 119 are still unACKed. So the sender starts the timer again.

63
00:08:27,687 --> 00:08:32,764
Then ACK 120 arrives. Send base becomes 120.

64
00:08:33,214 --> 00:08:36,926
Nothing is unACKed now, so no timer runs.

65
00:08:37,376 --> 00:08:46,912
Event 3 is a timeout. The sender retransmits the segment that caused the timeout, and restarts the timer. The next slides show this.

66
00:08:47,362 --> 00:08:57,282
This slide is optional. It is not on the section 003 slides. Slide 57 only names the interval, TimeOutInterval.

67
00:08:57,732 --> 00:09:08,249
The timer must be longer than the round trip time. If it is too short, premature timeouts occur. If it is too long, the sender reacts slowly to a loss.

68
00:09:08,699 --> 00:09:15,483
The R T T changes, so TCP measures it. Each measurement is a sample R T T.

69
00:09:15,933 --> 00:09:30,483
TCP keeps a moving average, the estimated R T T. The new value is 1 minus alpha, times the old value, plus alpha times the new sample. Alpha is typically point 1 2 5.

70
00:09:30,933 --> 00:09:38,250
Example: the old estimate is 100 milliseconds. The new sample is 120 milliseconds.

71
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:53,121
point 8 7 5 times 100 is 87 point 5. point 1 2 5 times 120 is 15. The sum, 102 point 5 milliseconds, is the new estimate.

72
00:09:53,571 --> 00:10:09,401
TCP also keeps the deviation, dev R T T. The new value is 1 minus beta, times the old value, plus beta times the distance between the sample and the estimate. Beta is typically point 2 5.

73
00:10:09,851 --> 00:10:21,136
The distance is 120 minus 102 point 5, which is 17 point 5 milliseconds. Here we use the new estimate, as the textbook does.

74
00:10:21,586 --> 00:10:38,354
The old dev R T T is 5. point 7 5 times 5 is 3 point 7 5. point 2 5 times 17 point 5 is 4 point 3 7 5. The sum is 8 point 1 2 5 milliseconds.

75
00:10:38,804 --> 00:10:50,068
The timeout interval is the estimated R T T plus 4 times dev R T T. 4 times 8 point 1 2 5 is 32 point 5.

76
00:10:50,518 --> 00:11:01,462
102 point 5 plus 32 point 5 is 135 milliseconds. A large variation gives a large safety margin.

77
00:11:01,912 --> 00:11:15,672
Slide 58 gives the ACK rules of the TCP receiver. There are two motivations. Motivation 1: send fewer small I P packets. Each separate ACK is its own I P packet.

78
00:11:16,122 --> 00:11:20,687
Motivation 2: deal with lost segments fast, for better throughput.

79
00:11:21,137 --> 00:11:31,079
So the receiver sometimes delays an ACK, and sometimes sends it immediately. It must not delay too much, or the sender's timer expires.

80
00:11:31,529 --> 00:11:44,713
Rule 1. An in-order segment arrives, and all earlier data is already ACKed. Here, the receiver expects byte 92, and sequence number 92 arrives with 8 bytes.

81
00:11:45,163 --> 00:11:53,355
The receiver delays the ACK. It waits up to 500 ms for the next segment. If none comes, it sends the ACK.

82
00:11:53,805 --> 00:12:05,837
Rule 2. An in-order segment arrives, and one other segment has an ACK pending. Here, sequence number 100 arrives in time. 100 is 92 plus 8.

83
00:12:06,287 --> 00:12:15,055
The receiver immediately sends one cumulative ACK for both segments: ACK 120. That is 100 plus 20.

84
00:12:15,505 --> 00:12:30,523
Rule 3. A segment arrives with a sequence number higher than expected. Here, 135 arrives, but 120 is expected. There is a gap, bytes 120 to 134.

85
00:12:30,973 --> 00:12:38,269
The receiver immediately sends a duplicate ACK, with the next expected byte: ACK 120 again.

86
00:12:38,719 --> 00:12:53,973
Rule 4. A segment arrives that partly or completely fills the gap, and starts at its lower end. Here, sequence number 120 with 15 bytes. It fills bytes 120 to 134.

87
00:12:54,423 --> 00:13:06,753
Bytes 135 to 144 are already in the buffer. So the receiver immediately sends ACK 145. That is 135 plus 10.

88
00:13:07,203 --> 00:13:11,406
Rules 1 and 2 serve motivation 1: fewer ACK packets.

89
00:13:11,856 --> 00:13:17,339
Rules 3 and 4 serve motivation 2: a fast reaction to lost segments.

90
00:13:17,789 --> 00:13:27,815
Slide 59 shows two retransmission scenarios. The first is a lost ACK. Host A sends sequence number 92 with 8 bytes.

91
00:13:28,265 --> 00:13:31,849
Host A starts the timer. Send base is 92.

92
00:13:32,299 --> 00:13:40,854
Host B receives bytes 92 to 99. It sends ACK 100, because 92 plus 8 is 100.

93
00:13:41,304 --> 00:13:43,075
The network loses this ACK.

94
00:13:43,525 --> 00:13:46,639
Host A gets no ACK. The timer expires.

95
00:13:47,089 --> 00:13:51,804
Host A retransmits sequence number 92, and restarts the timer.

96
00:13:52,254 --> 00:14:01,534
Host B already has bytes 92 to 99. So its next expected byte stays 100, and it sends ACK 100 again.

97
00:14:01,984 --> 00:14:04,928
Now host A sets send base to 100.

98
00:14:05,378 --> 00:14:11,735
The sender cannot tell a lost segment from a lost ACK. So it retransmits after every timeout.

99
00:14:12,185 --> 00:14:23,983
The second scenario of slide 59 is a premature timeout. Host A sends sequence number 92 with 8 bytes, then sequence number 100 with 20 bytes.

100
00:14:24,433 --> 00:14:29,297
One timer runs, for 92. In this scenario, nothing is lost.

101
00:14:29,747 --> 00:14:35,123
Host B sends ACK 100 and ACK 120. But the ACKs are slow.

102
00:14:35,573 --> 00:14:38,922
The timer expires before ACK 100 arrives.

103
00:14:39,372 --> 00:14:46,177
So host A retransmits sequence number 92. This timeout is too early: it is premature.

104
00:14:46,627 --> 00:14:51,043
Then ACK 100 arrives, and send base becomes 100.

105
00:14:51,493 --> 00:14:56,421
ACK 120 arrives, and send base becomes 120.

106
00:14:56,871 --> 00:15:03,826
The copy of segment 92 reaches host B. Host B already expects byte 120.

107
00:15:04,276 --> 00:15:11,017
So host B sends the cumulative ACK 120. Send base stays 120.

108
00:15:11,467 --> 00:15:15,542
The retransmission was not necessary, but it causes no error.

109
00:15:15,992 --> 00:15:25,613
Slide 60 shows a third scenario. Host A sends sequence number 92 with 8 bytes, and sequence number 100 with 20 bytes.

110
00:15:26,063 --> 00:15:28,346
The network loses ACK 100.

111
00:15:28,796 --> 00:15:32,977
But ACK 120 arrives before the timer expires.

112
00:15:33,427 --> 00:15:44,883
ACK 120 means that all bytes up to 119 arrived. This includes bytes 92 to 99. So send base becomes 120.

113
00:15:45,333 --> 00:15:52,587
Host A does not retransmit. It sends new data: sequence number 120 with 15 bytes.

114
00:15:53,037 --> 00:15:56,322
The cumulative ACK covers for the earlier lost ACK.

115
00:15:56,772 --> 00:16:06,820
Slide 61 gives fast retransmit. The motivation: if the sender gets an indication of a loss, it does not need to wait for the timeout.

116
00:16:07,270 --> 00:16:13,905
Here, host A sends five segments. The sizes of the last three segments are added for this example.

117
00:16:14,355 --> 00:16:17,555
The segment with sequence number 100 is lost.

118
00:16:18,005 --> 00:16:26,730
Segment 92 arrives. Host B sends ACK 100. This is the first ACK 100. It is not a duplicate.

119
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:29,185
Send base becomes 100.

120
00:16:29,635 --> 00:16:40,899
Segments 120, 135, and 145 arrive after the gap. For each one, rule 3 gives an ACK 100 again.

121
00:16:41,349 --> 00:16:43,867
So host A gets three duplicate ACKs.

122
00:16:44,317 --> 00:16:56,903
The rule: if the sender receives 3 additional ACKs, 4 in total, for the same data, it resends the unACKed segment with the smallest sequence number. That is 100.

123
00:16:57,353 --> 00:17:00,852
It does this right away. The timer has not expired.

124
00:17:01,302 --> 00:17:16,065
Segment 100 fills the gap at host B. Bytes 120 to 149 are already in the buffer. So rule 4 gives ACK 150, which is 145 plus 5.

125
00:17:16,515 --> 00:17:23,171
Three duplicate ACKs mean that three segments arrived after a missing segment. So a loss is likely.

126
00:17:23,621 --> 00:17:39,215
Now the question bank. Question 37 asks for the purpose of each field in the TCP header. You can ignore the U, P, C and E bits and the urgent pointer. Pause the video and try it. Draw the header first.

127
00:17:39,665 --> 00:17:44,252
One: the ports identify the sending socket and the receiving socket.

128
00:17:44,702 --> 00:17:49,097
Two: the sequence number is the byte number of the first data byte.

129
00:17:49,547 --> 00:17:54,453
Three: the acknowledgement number is the next byte expected from the other side.

130
00:17:54,903 --> 00:17:58,829
Four: head len is the header length, in units of 4 bytes.

131
00:17:59,279 --> 00:18:05,529
Five: the A bit says that the ACK field is valid. Reset, SYN and FIN manage the connection.

132
00:18:05,979 --> 00:18:12,571
Six: the receive window is the number of bytes that the receiver will accept. It is for flow control.

133
00:18:13,021 --> 00:18:15,603
Seven: the checksum detects bit errors.

134
00:18:16,053 --> 00:18:19,210
Eight: the options, for example the MSS.

135
00:18:19,660 --> 00:18:22,007
Nine: the data from the application.

136
00:18:22,457 --> 00:18:28,963
Question 38 asks for the four ACK rules and their motivations. Pause the video and try it.

137
00:18:29,413 --> 00:18:38,288
Rule 1: an in-order segment arrives, and all earlier data is ACKed. Delay the ACK, up to 500 ms.

138
00:18:38,738 --> 00:18:46,631
Rule 2: an in-order segment arrives, and one ACK is pending. Send one cumulative ACK for both, immediately.

139
00:18:47,081 --> 00:18:53,865
Rules 1 and 2 serve motivation 1: fewer small I P packets, for router efficiency.

140
00:18:54,315 --> 00:19:02,977
Rule 3: an out-of-order segment arrives, so there is a gap. Immediately send a duplicate ACK with the next expected byte.

141
00:19:03,427 --> 00:19:09,528
Rule 4: a segment fills the gap and starts at its lower end. Immediately send an ACK.

142
00:19:09,978 --> 00:19:21,285
Rules 3 and 4 serve motivation 2: deal with lost segments fast, for better throughput. The delay must stay short, so that the sender's timer does not expire.

143
00:19:21,735 --> 00:19:30,396
Question 39 asks for the two conditions that trigger a retransmission at the TCP sender. Pause the video and try it.

144
00:19:30,846 --> 00:19:37,950
Condition 1 is a timeout. The R T O timer of the oldest unACKed segment expires.

145
00:19:38,400 --> 00:19:42,304
The sender retransmits that segment, and restarts the timer.

146
00:19:42,754 --> 00:19:53,762
Condition 2 is triple duplicate ACKs. Segment 100 is lost. The sender gets 3 additional ACKs 100, 4 in total, for the same data.

147
00:19:54,212 --> 00:20:03,364
It retransmits the unACKed segment with the smallest sequence number, 100, right away. This is fast retransmit.

148
00:20:03,814 --> 00:20:13,905
Here is the recap. One. The sequence number is the number of the first data byte. The ACK number is the next expected byte. It is cumulative.

149
00:20:14,355 --> 00:20:20,200
Two. Each direction has its own byte numbers. One segment can carry data and an ACK.

150
00:20:20,650 --> 00:20:27,754
Three. The sender keeps one timer, for the oldest unACKed segment. A new ACK moves send base.

151
00:20:28,204 --> 00:20:41,879
Four. The receiver delays an in-order ACK up to 500 ms. It ACKs two in-order segments at once. It sends a duplicate ACK when it sees a gap, and an ACK at once when the gap fills.

152
00:20:42,329 --> 00:20:55,171
Five. The sender retransmits after a timeout, or after three duplicate ACKs. That is fast retransmit. Video 10 b continues with flow control and connection management.
